Appendix A — Interview transcripts
Car owners were asked eight core questions: (1) how they track maintenance, (2) their last car problem, (3) how they judge urgency, (4) whether they have felt overcharged, (5) whether they would trust an AI advisor, (6) what they want reminders for, (7) which apps they use now, and (8) what would stop them using the app.
Interview 1 — Basmalah
1.	I set reminders on my phone and keep track of the mileage. I sometimes go overdue for oil changes until my dad reminds me.
2.	A couple of months ago, I smelled burning in my car and noticed the temperature gauge was on H. I called my brother; he told me to pull over and called a recovery truck.
3.	I don't know. If something shows up on the dashboard, I call my brother, and he tells me what to do.
4.	Yes, all the time, especially at a workshop I have not been to before.
5.	Not really. It would have to agree with what my brother or dad tells me, consistently, for a very long time before I count on it.
6.	All the regular and big maintenance jobs, the best time to do them, and if I can't do one, how long I can put it off before it becomes a problem.
7.	No, I only use my calendar app for reminders.
8.	It has to be free, with good privacy for my name and car type. It should be easy to navigate and make it clear whether a problem is big or small.
Interview 2 — Mohammed
1.	I have a contract with my trusted workshop, and they remind me when service is due. Occasionally, when I'm busy with work, I'm late, but not by more than a couple of weeks.
2.	Getting on the highway, I felt a drop in power. I pressed harder, but the car barely went faster; then the check-engine light and many other warnings came on. I pulled over and called my workshop, which sent a recovery truck. The turbo was damaged.
3.	I rely on what the workshop tells me, nothing else.
4.	No, I service my car regularly at that workshop, and they are honest. But it took a lot of time, going through mechanics who lie and charge for parts you don't need, before I found them.
5.	Yes, especially if my workshop gives it the thumbs up.
6.	Everything one needs reminding of, like how much life my brake pads have left or when to change my gear oil. It should be personalized to my car; many apps I tried don't have my car.
7.	No, because they don't list my car. Peugeot is fairly rare here; you find the most popular cars but not every car.
8.	Cost. The app has to be free for me to even consider it. Otherwise, no problem.
Additional comment: make sure the app has a wide variety of cars.
Interview 3 — Abdulla
1.	I keep a physical booklet for my car and track everything in it. Sometimes I'm late by a few days, but otherwise never.
2.	About two weeks ago, the car kept pulling to the left, and I had to hold the steering wheel to the right. I pulled over and found I had a flat tire from a nail. I fitted the spare and had the tire repaired at the ADNOC station on the Corniche.
3.	I know cars well and trust my knowledge. By listening for strange sounds and checking the gauges, I can tell if there is a problem.
4.	Sometimes, but I also know parts for my car are usually expensive because it's a rare car.
5.	I would first test it with a few questions I already know the answers to. If it passes, I can trust it to confirm what I already suspect.
6.	If it can link up with Google reminders, I'd love that.
7.	I use a physical booklet, with an app called VINwiki as a backup to record work when it's done.
8.	Cost. If the app costs money, it had better be exceptional.
Additional comment: include an FAQ for specific cars. For example, on my car the timing belt must be replaced every 100,000 km.
Interview 4 — Praveen (mechanic, Mussafah)
1.	What do customers most misunderstand about warning signs? They don't understand how serious some warning lights are, and sometimes it's the opposite: they see a tire-pressure light and send the car on a recovery truck.
2.	Which symptoms are ignored or overreacted to? High engine temperature: people think driving slower with a hot engine causes no damage, which is very wrong. The check engine light: they think the engine will explode, when it can be as simple as an oxygen sensor.
3.	What do customers misunderstand about cost? They think asking for a lower price will get it. I don't control parts prices; I buy the parts and fit them. They always ask for discounts on parts and labor, but then how do we make money?
4.	What should the app include? Display parts prices and show customers how much it will cost to fix their car.
Interview 5 — Mahmoud (used-car dealer)
1.	How do you check a used car's condition? We do a PPI (pre-purchase inspection), checking everything from engine health to the gearbox and chassis. We also check the mileage stored in the car's computer and compare it with the odometer.
2.	What information is hardest to get? A used car's previous history is very difficult to access. If there are no physical service records in the car, you really don't know what it has been through.


